menu "Yaykit cloud"

config YK_CLOUD_ORIGIN
    string "Device API origin"
    default "https://device.yaykit.io"
    help
        Origin of the device API (services/device). Yaykit has one environment since 2026-10-17. Until
        image signing ships (docs/architecture/device-platform.md 6.2), cloud firmware goes only to
        internal test builds, not public releases.

config YK_REGIONS
    bool "Choose the deployment by the device's region (two deployments)"
    default n
    help
        Two deployments, elsewhere (global) and mainland China (cn), from one firmware (docs/delivery/
        2026-11-20-两套部署-总纲.md; the firmware's plan 4.1-4.5). The addresses are the contract's region table,
        include/yk_regions_gen.h (pnpm contracts:sync writes it), which only yk_region.c reads. On, the device's
        region is yk_acct `rg` as stored, read once at start: no `rg`, or a name not in the table, is row 0, global.
        yk_cloud_origin() and yk_session_url() (yaykit_session) are then that row's addresses; global's stay
        CONFIG_YK_CLOUD_ORIGIN and YK_SESSION_URL, so a development build's own origin still works.
        Off (the default, every firmware until the release that turns it on): always global, nothing read from NVS,
        the addresses exactly today's. This setting writes no `rg`: activation by the claim's prefix does, later.

config YK_CLOUD_HTTP_BUFFER_MIN
    int "Smallest HTTP buffer, in bytes"
    default 0
    help
        An HTTPS client's receive and send buffers are at least this. 0 leaves them as written (2 KB or less, in internal
        RAM). Set it above CONFIG_SPIRAM_MALLOC_ALWAYSINTERNAL to have them in PSRAM: about 3.5 KB less internal RAM for
        each request. With a limit above it (16384, some firmware here) they stay internal and only grow.

endmenu
